 3$ and $\\gamma_R = 4$, the pattern seems to be: radius ≤  romanDominationNumber - 1.\n\n**Formalizing and Proving the Radius Bound**\n\nThe general conjecture is that the `radius(G) ≤ romanDominationNumber(G) - 1`. The goal is to mathematically formalize and prove the general case of  `radius G ≤ romanDominationNumber G - 1`.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing the Cases Based on V2 Cardinality**\n\nConsidering the case where the set of vertices with value 2 ($V_2$) has cardinality 0. Then all vertices must have a value of 1, implying the total Roman weight equals the number of vertices ($W = n$). Connectedness implies the radius is at most $n - 1 = W - 1$, thus, $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le W$.  If the cardinality of $V_2$ is 1, then the Roman weight is $m + 2$. The radius is at most $m + 1$, so again the inequality holds: $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le W$.\n\n**Developing a Strategy for k ≥ 2 Case**\n\nFocusing on the case where $|V_2| \\ge 2$, consider a minimal spanning tree, T, spanning the vertices in $V_2$. The leaves of this tree must belong to $V_2$, and any other vertex in T must either be in V0 or V1. Two consecutive vertices in the tree cannot both be in V0.\n\n**Proving Radius Bound for |V2| ≥ 2**\n\nIf x and y in V0 are adjacent, they are adjacent to vertices in V2. Examining edges within the tree spanning $V_2$ will potentially establish bounds on distances and thereby radius. This suggests the bound holds more generally.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_subagents","args":{"Action":"\"list\"","toolAction":"\"Analyzing directory\"","toolSummary":"\"List subagents\""}}]}
